<p>
A while ago, a new site popped up in my feed. That site was <a href="https://practical.engineering/blog">Practical Engineering</a>. I didn&rsquo;t know how it got into my feed until I started to write this post. When I checked, I discovered that I was getting it from <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/">Hacker News</a>. In any event, it&rsquo;s a site that most Irreal readers will probably love.
</p>
<p>
Each post looks at some aspect of our life that is impacted by engineering. For example, the latest one to pop up was about <a href="https://practical.engineering/blog/2019/5/11/how-do-traffic-signals-work">traffic signals</a>. You wouldn&rsquo;t think there&rsquo;s a lot to know but it turns out that there are significant engineering problems to be solved to get cars from one place to another.
</p>
<p>
A previous, somewhat related post dealt with <a href="https://practical.engineering/blog/2023/12/29/how-railroad-crossings-work?rq=railroad">how railroad crossings work</a>. Again, you wouldn&rsquo;t think there&rsquo;s much to the topic but it turns out that the problem is much more complex than you think. Or there&rsquo;s the post that asks <a href="https://practical.engineering/blog/2026/8/18/are-beavers-actually-good-at-dams">Are Beavers Actually Good at Dams?</a> (Spoiler: yes)
</p>
<p>
The posts are actually transcripts of videos from Grady Hillhouse, a civil engineer. Each post includes the video so you can simply watch them if you prefer but it&rsquo;s informative to use them both. I&rsquo;ve watched/read several of them and found them all entertaining and informative. They opened my eyes to some of the complexities behind everyday phenomena.
</p>
<p>
Sadly, as far as I can see, the site doesn&rsquo;t have an RSS feed so you have to depend on something thing Hacker News to inform you of new posts. There is an <a href="https://practical.engineering/email-list">email list</a> that will let you know when there are new videos but an RSS feed would be a definite improvement. Take a look at a couple of the posts if you haven&rsquo;t seen them before.</p>
